;; Local state: a function-local `atom` as a value that is REBOUND, not a cell
;; that is mutated. This is the second widening `surface-status.edn`
;; `:no-ambient-mutation` names, under `:local-atom-elaboration`.
;;
;; The invariant that entry protects is that state must not be AMBIENT -- not
;; that mutation is forbidden. Two shapes follow from reading it that way, and
;; until 2026-09-02 only one of them had an answer:
;;
;;   state that ESCAPES or PERSISTS   provider-owned; :state capability kit,
;;                                    grant at instantiation, effect row shows
;;                                    it. That is :state-kit-desugar, and it is
;;                                    NOT what this file is about.
;;
;;   state that does neither          (let [a (atom 0)] (swap! a + 1) @a)
;;                                    needs no host, no grant, no runtime cell.
;;                                    Every write is visible to the compiler,
;;                                    so it can be compiled away entirely.
;;
;; Unison's `Ref` is the shape; unlike Unison's, this one has no ability and no
;; handler, because it does not survive compilation. Slice 1 admits exactly the
;; second shape and refuses, by name, every way the first could be reached.
{:kotoba.lang.local-state/version 1
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/status :slice-1-implemented
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/as-of "2026-09-02"
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/implementation
 "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema kotoba.compiler.frontend (elaborate-local-state)"
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/decided-by
 "adr-2608301500-guest-syntax-restrictions-classified-by-shielding-mechanism"
 :kotoba.lang.local-state/owner-decision
 "2026-09-02: build it, fail-closed, in slices"

 :invariant
 "no ambient state: a cell exists only as a rebinding inside one function body, so nothing outside that body can name, hold, store or observe it -- and nothing exists at runtime to observe"

 ;; Nothing. The cell has no runtime representation and no authority is
 ;; involved, so there is nothing to declare, grant or attenuate. This is the
 ;; whole difference from the :state kit, which has a wire id (8) because a
 ;; host owns the store.
 :effect-row-member nil
 :named-operation :local-state
 :named-operation-why
 "An elaboration that adds nothing to the effect row would otherwise be invisible in check output and provenance. :named-operations is where a reader sees it happened."

 :surface #{atom swap! reset! deref}

 :semantics
 {:atom
  {:form "(atom init)"
   :admitted-position "the init expression of a let binding, and only there"
   :binds "a CELL, not a value. The name is not a lexical value binding: it can be spelled only as the first argument of swap!/reset!/deref."
   :type "the cell's type is init's type, and every rebinding must agree with it"
   :scope "the let that binds it; the cell dies with that let"}
  :swap!
  {:form "(swap! a f x y ...)"
   :elaborates-to "(f a x y ...) under the ordinary arity limits, where `a` is the symbol currently holding the cell's value"
   :head-rule "a lexical symbol head is ordinary application; a computed head follows the existing invoke rule"
   :value "the NEW value, as Clojure's swap! returns"
   :argument-rule "arguments may READ the cell (they see the value before this write) but may not write one; a write inside another write's arguments is refused"}
  :reset!
  {:form "(reset! a v)"
   :elaborates-to "a new binding of v"
   :value "the new value, as Clojure's reset! returns"}
  :deref
  {:form "(deref a)"
   :sugar "@a, which reads as (clojure.core/deref a) on the JVM reader and is normalised"
   :elaborates-to "the symbol currently holding the cell's value"
   :value "the current value"}}

 ;; The load-bearing rule. Everything else is mechanism; this is what makes the
 ;; state non-ambient rather than merely convenient.
 :escape-rule
 {:statement "a cell may appear ONLY as the first argument of swap!/reset!/deref, in the function body that binds it"
  :refused #{:passed-as-an-argument
             :returned
             :stored-in-a-collection-or-record
             :captured-by-a-fn-literal
             :read-inside-loop-recur-doseq-dotimes-or-a-lazy-thunk
             :named-in-another-function
             :shadowed-by-a-binding-of-the-same-name}
  :message "atom `a` escapes its let scope (atom slice 1 admits swap!/reset!/deref in straight-line code of the binding function only)"
  :why "If a cell could be named anywhere else, the elaboration would be a mutation feature rather than a widening of :no-ambient-mutation. A cell nobody can hold needs no grant precisely because nobody can hold it."}

 :elaboration
 {:model :state-passing
  :where "on SOURCE, per function body, before desugaring -- so the rest of the pipeline reads ordinary let/if"
  :straight-line "each write emits one new let binding; deref reads the current binding"
  :admitted-control #{let do if when cond case}
  :branch-join
  {:encoding :one-copy-per-mutated-cell
   :statement "a branching form whose arms write is emitted once per mutated cell, projecting that cell's post-branch value, plus once for the form's own value; the enclosing scope rebinds each cell from its copy"
   :why-not-a-pair "The alternative was to thread a tuple of (value, cells) through. `pair` in this language is i64-only (kotoba.compiler.frontend heap-operations), so a tuple encoding would have restricted cells to i64. Copying the arm does not."
   :why-copying-is-sound "Kotoba branches are pure: the copies compute the same values and differ only in which one they project. An arm that aborts aborts in the first copy, at the same point."
   :the-one-exception "a capability call. Copying it would duplicate the effect, so an arm that both writes a cell and calls a capability is refused."
   :cost "(mutated cells + 1) copies per branching form, bounded by the existing :max-lowered-nodes budget"
   :scrutinee "bound once before the copies, so a case/if test is still evaluated exactly once"}
  :scoped-binding
  {:statement "an ordinary let binding inside a cell's scope opens a scope the rebinding chain cannot leave, so it follows the same copy rule: entered once per mutated cell and once for the value"
   :why "the alternative was to flatten or alpha-rename the inner let, and renaming user binders would have to know every binding position in the grammar"}
  :byte-identity
  {:statement "a body that mentions none of atom/swap!/reset!/deref is returned unchanged, so no other program's lowering moves"
   :pinned-by "kotoba-sema test/kotoba/compiler/local_state_test.clj -- HIR and KIR sha256 of an atom-free program, taken on 411d4cab before the pass existed"}}

 :types
 {:cell-type "the init's type"
  :rebinding-rule "every swap!/reset! result must unify with it"
  :checked-where "kotoba.compiler.frontend/local-state-check-cell-type!, in the let case of type inference -- the cell's identity survives desugaring in the binding NAME, and the elaboration itself has no types yet"
  :message "atom `a` is i64; this rebinding is string (atom slice 1 requires one type per cell)"
  :not-restricted-to-i64 "a cell may hold any admitted value type; a string cell is exercised in the tests"}

 ;; What slice 1 does NOT do, so a reader does not have to infer it from the
 ;; refusals. Each of these stays on the :state-kit-desugar path.
 :out-of-scope
 {:escaping-atoms "an atom passed, returned or stored -- host kit + grant"
  :persisting-atoms "state that outlives one call -- host kit + grant"
  :cross-function-atoms "an atom shared by two functions -- host kit + grant"
  :ref-dosync-volatile-binding-var-set! "no ability model decided; still refused by the head"
  :writes-under-iteration "a write inside loop/doseq/dotimes: the body runs a number of times the rebinding chain cannot name. A future slice may admit it by threading the cell through the loop's own accumulator."
  :writes-in-short-circuiting-heads "and / or / some-> / cond-> / condp / match / -> : hoisting a write out of one would make it unconditional, so it is refused rather than silently promoted"}

 :refusals
 {:escape "atom `a` escapes its let scope (atom slice 1 admits swap!/reset!/deref in straight-line code of the binding function only)"
  :atom-position "atom must be the init expression of a let binding (atom slice 1 admits swap!/reset!/deref in straight-line code of the binding function only)"
  :not-a-cell "<op> expects a let-bound atom cell as its first argument; got `x` (atom slice 1)"
  :position "swap!/reset! is not admitted inside `<head>` (atom slice 1 admits them in let, do, if, when, cond and case only)"
  :cell-type "atom `a` is i64; this rebinding is string (atom slice 1 requires one type per cell)"
  :effectful-branch "a branch that writes an atom must not contain a capability call (atom slice 1 elaborates the branch once per cell)"
  :cond-without-else "a cond that writes an atom must end with :else (atom slice 1 admits swap!/reset!/deref in straight-line code of the binding function only)"
  :shadow "atom `a` shadows an atom of the same name (atom slice 1 admits swap!/reset!/deref in straight-line code of the binding function only)"}

 :error-codes #{:kotoba.error/local-state-escape
                :kotoba.error/local-state-atom-position
                :kotoba.error/local-state-not-a-cell
                :kotoba.error/local-state-position
                :kotoba.error/local-state-cell-type
                :kotoba.error/local-state-shadow
                :kotoba.error/local-state-arity
                :kotoba.error/local-state-effectful-branch}

 :conformance
 {:positive [:local-state-slice-1-counter-in-a-let
             :local-state-slice-1-atom-through-an-if
             :local-state-slice-1-swap-with-extra-arguments]
  :negative [:local-state-slice-1-negative-escapes-as-an-argument
             :local-state-slice-1-negative-escapes-into-a-fn
             :local-state-slice-1-negative-read-inside-a-loop
             :local-state-slice-1-negative-swap-changes-the-type
             :local-state-slice-1-negative-deref-of-a-non-cell]
  :executed-by "test/kotoba/lang/local_state_conformance_test.clj; negatives compared with = so a refusal for another reason cannot count"}

 :backends
 ;; The elaboration is a frontend rewrite into forms every backend already
 ;; carries, so there is no per-backend lowering to qualify. What has been
 ;; MEASURED is the KIR reference; the others follow from the fact that the
 ;; emitted program contains no new head, but that is an inference, not a run.
 {:measured #{:kir}
  :inference "the elaborated program uses only let/if/case/do and ordinary calls, which every backend already lowers"
  :not-measured #{:wasm32-kotoba-v1 :js-kotoba-v1 :native-x86_64 :native-aarch64}}}
